## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates practical, beginner-appropriate streaming ingestion using common AWS data tooling and basic quality controls.

## Key Concepts
- Kinesis Data Streams for real-time data ingestion
- AWS Glue ETL and Data Catalog for data processing and metadata management
- Parquet format with date-based partitioning for optimized storage and queries
- Simple schema evolution strategies for handling changing data structures
- Data quality checks (required fields, types, nulls)
- CloudWatch alarms and end-to-end testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code to start a Glue job
const glue = new AWS.Glue();
await glue.startJobRun({
  JobName: 'mobile-events-etl',
  Arguments: {
    '--input-stream': 'mobile-app-events',
    '--output-path': 's3://analytics-bucket/events/',
    '--partition-format': 'yyyy/MM/dd'
  }
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
Practical beginner task validating end-to-end ingestion, data quality, and cataloging.

## Key Concepts
- S3 event-driven ETL
- Glue Spark/DynamicFrame validation
- Quarantine routing for bad records
- Parquet partitioning by date
- Glue Data Catalog and Athena access

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo Glue-like JS snippet for validation
function process(batch){
  const valid=[], quarantined=[];
  for (const r of batch){
    if (r.a != null && r.b != null) valid.push(transform(r));
    else quarantined.push({row:r, validation_reason:'missing_required_fields'});
  }
  writeParquet(valid, datePartition);
  writeJson(quarantined, quarantineBucket);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test schema evolution if new fields are added?
- How would you monitor the quarantine rate and alert if it spikes?
